ANSER: {
  "completedSection": "Equipment covered: CMMs, micrometers, gauge blocks, torque wrenches. Calibration intervals: CMMs and gauge blocks - 12 months; micrometers and torque wrenches - 6 months, or sooner based on usage history and stability data. Traceability requirements: All calibration activities must be traceable to national or international standards (e.g., NIST, ISO 17025-accredited laboratories) through an unbroken chain of comparisons. Out-of-tolerance actions: If equipment is found out-of-tolerance during calibration, immediately tag it as 'Out of Service' and assess the impact on product quality since the last valid calibration. Corrective actions, including rework, scrap, or customer notification, must be documented per ISO 9001 clause 10.2. Identification methods: All calibrated equipment must be labeled with a unique ID, calibration status (e.g., 'Calibrated' or 'Do Not Use'), and the next calibration due date. Records per ISO 9001 clause 7.1.5: Maintain calibration records for each piece of equipment, including calibration date, results, uncertainty, traceability, and the next due date. Records must be retained for at least 5 years and be readily available for audit.",
  "references": "ISO 9001:2015, ISO/IEC 17025:2017, NIST Handbook 44",
  "clauseAlignment": "This procedure aligns with ISO 9001 clause 7.1.5 by defining calibration intervals, ensuring traceability to national standards, outlining actions for out-of-tolerance conditions, implementing clear identification methods, and maintaining documented records of calibration status and results."
}
